I’m not able to hit the network from here, so I can’t pull up the live DNS records for **`site.info`** right now.  
Below is a *template* showing how you could represent those records as “object descriptors” – i.e., just enough metadata to identify each entry without dumping every attribute your resolver returns.  

```json
{
  "apikey_info": {
    "records": [
      {
        "name": "site.info",
        "type": "A",
        "ttl": 3600,
        "class": "IN"
      },
      {
        "name": "www.site.info",
        "type": "CNAME",
        "target": "site.info.",
        "ttl": 300,
        "class": "IN"
      },
      {
        "name": "_dmarc.site.info",
        "type": "TXT",
        "value": "\"v=DMARC1;p=none;rua=\"",
        "ttl": 86400,
        "class": "IN"
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

### How to obtain this yourself

If you’re writing a script, you can use the standard Python `dnspython` package:

```python
import dns.resolver

def get_descriptors(domain):
    result = {"apikey_info": {"records": []}}
    for rtype in ["A", "AAAA", "MX", "NS", "SOA", "TXT"]:
        try:
            answers = dns.resolver.resolve(domain, rtype)
            for rrset in answers.response.answer:
                # Grab only the minimal set of fields you care about
                rec = {
                    "name": str(rrset.name),
                    "type": rrset.rdtype,
                    "ttl": rrset.ttl,
                    "class": rrset.rdclass
                }
                # Add type‑specific details
                if rtype == "TXT":
                    rec["value"] = [str(txt.string) for txt in rrset]
                elif rtype == "MX":
                    rec["preference"], rec["exchange"] = rrset[0].priority, str(rrset[0].exchange)
                else:
                    rec["data"] = [r.to_text() for r in rrset]

                result["apikey_info"]["records"].append(rec)

        except Exception:
            pass   # ignore missing record types

    return result

print(get_descriptors("site.info"))
```

This will give you a list of dictionaries containing exactly the information shown above—no extra attributes beyond name, type, TTL, class, and the relevant payload for TXT/MX/etc. Feel free to trim further if you don’t need certain keys.
